% php: the pigeon-hole principle, H holes and P pigeons; P > H is
% UNSAT (no way to place every pigeon), P <= H is SAT.  P variables,
% one per pigeon, each with the domain 1..H -- the pigeon's hole.
% The all-different requirement is expressed, without loss of
% generality, as the strict chain V[1] #< V[2] #< ...: an assignment
% with distinct holes exists iff a sorted one does, and a sorted one
% needs P distinct values out of H.  The constraint loop sits inside
% the if-condition: on cp the constraints propagate eagerly at posting
% time, so on the UNSAT side the posting itself fails and takes the
% UNSAT branch (on pb and sat the solve fails instead).
% Sized by the command line:
%   picat php.pi <holes> <pigeons>
% The same program body runs unchanged under import pb, import cp and
% import sat; only the import line decides the solver.  pb sends the
% cardinality form to an external pseudo-Boolean solver (env PBSOL);
% cp and sat are built in.  No timing here -- see php_bench.pi.
%
% With pb:   PBSOL=<solver> PICATPATH=<picat>/lib2 emu/picat php.pi 8 9
% With cp:   emu/picat php.pi 8 9            (after the import swap)

import pb, os.

main([HolesS, PigeonsS]) =>
    H = HolesS.to_int,
    P = PigeonsS.to_int,
    V = new_array(P),
    V :: 1..H,
    ( (foreach (I in 1..P - 1)
           V[I] #< V[I + 1]
       end,
       solve(V)) ->
        printf("SAT: a placement exists (%d pigeons, %d holes)%n", P, H)
    ;
        printf("UNSAT: %d pigeons cannot fit into %d holes%n", P, H)
    ).
